'''''Trifolium vesiculosum''''', the '''arrowleaf clover''', is a species of flowering plant in the family [[Fabaceae]].<ref name="1_of_1820_Google_Scholar_results" >{{cite journal |title=Management Effects on Forage Production and Digestibility of 'Yuchi' Arrowleaf Clover ( ''Trifolium vesiculosum'' Savi)<sup>1</sup> |date=1970 |last1=Hoveland |first1=C. S. |last2=Carden |first2=E. L. |last3=Anthony |first3=W. B. |last4=Cunningham |first4=J. P. |journal=Agronomy Journal |volume=62 |pages=115–116 |doi=10.2134/agronj1970.00021962006200010037x }}</ref> It is native to southeastern and eastern Europe, the northern Caucasus, Turkey, Syria, and Lebanon, and has been introduced to other locales, including the United States and Australia.<ref name="POWO_523840-1" >{{cite POWO |id=523840-1 |title=''Trifolium vesiculosum'' Savi |access-date=23 September 2023 }}</ref> A cool-season annual, it is a large species reaching {{cvt|1|m|ft|0}} with {{cvt|5|cm|0}} leaves.<ref name="1_of_1820_Google_Scholar_results" /><ref name="2_of_1820_Google_Scholar_results" >{{cite journal |last1=Ovalle |first1=Carlos |last2=del Pozo |first2=Alejandro |last3=Fernández |first3=Fernando |last4=Chavarría |first4=Jorge |last5=Arredondo |first5=Susana |date=January–March 2010 |title=Arrowleaf clover (''Trifolium vesiculosum'' Savi): A new species of annual legumes for high rainfall areas of the Mediterranean climate zone of Chile |journal=Chilean Journal of Agricultural Research |volume=70 |issue=1 |pages=170–177 |doi=10.4067/S0718-58392010000100018 |doi-access=free |hdl=1807/45804 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>
